\section s_Malterlib_Core_CodeStandard_Naming_TypePrefixes Type prefixes

Types, enumerators and namespaces are prefixed.

<table>
<tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Usage</th><th>Examples</th><th>Discussion</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td>E</td>
	<td>Enums and enumerators</td>
	<td>
~~~
	enum ETest
	{
		ETest_Value = 0
	};
~~~
	</td>
	<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>N</td>
	<td>Namespaces</td>
	<td>
~~~
	namespace NTest
	{
	}
~~~
	</td>
	<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>C</td>
	<td>Classes, structs, typedefs and any other type not specified here</td>
	<td>
~~~
	class CTest
	{
	};
	typedef int CTestInt;
~~~
	</td>
	<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>IC</td>
	<td>Interface classes</td>
	<td>
~~~
	struct ICTest
	{ 
		virtual void f_Function() abstract; 
	};
~~~
	</td>
	<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>F</td>
	<td>Function types</td>
	<td>
~~~
	typedef void (FTest)();
	FTest* fTest = []{};
~~~
	</td>
	<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>TC</td>
	<td>Template classes, structs and any other template type not specified here</td>
	<td>
~~~
	template <typename t_CType>
	struct TCTest
	{
	};
~~~
	</td>
	<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>TIC</td>
	<td>Template interface classes</td>
	<td>
~~~
	template <typename t_CType>
	struct TICTest
	{
		virtual void f_Function() abstract; 
	};
~~~
	</td>
	<td></td>
</tr>
</table>
 

\section s_Malterlib_Core_CodeStandard_Naming_ConceptPrefixes Concept prefixes

The concept prefix is intended to gives us information about a variable or parameter at a glance. Only a few highly used concepts have prefixes. The prefix goes after the storage prefix and before the name.

Keep in mind that the concept is not necessarily a reflection of the type of the variable, but actually what concept the variable represents.

If something models several concepts, only the most important or 'outer' concept is used for the prefix. For example a pointer to a boolean would be prefixed with 'p' only and not 'pb'. The reason for not adding several prefixes is to keep the rules simple and rather expose this complexity by explicitly putting this information in the variable name.

Function return values should not be concept prefixed.

<table>
<tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Usage</th><th>Examples</th><th>Discussion</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td>b</td>
	<td>Boolean</td>
	<td>
~~~
	bool bTest = true;
	void (bool _bTest);
	struct CTest
	{
		bool m_bTest;
		uint32 m_bTest:1;
		uint32 m_bTest2:2;
	};
~~~
	</td>
	<td>A boolean is any value that is used like a boolean. This includes things with bool type and for example booleans saved in a bitfield.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>i</td>
	<td>Iterator or index</td>
	<td>
~~~
	TCVector<uint8> ByteBuffer;
	auto rByte = fg_Range(ByteBuffer);
	for (auto iByte = rByte.f_Front(); iByte != rByte.f_Back(); ++iByte)
		*iByte;

	for (mint iByte = 0; iByte < ByteBuffer.f_GetLen(); ++iByte)
		ByteBuffer[iByte];
~~~
	</td>
	<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>n</td>
	<td>Number of</td>
	<td>
~~~
	TCVector<uint8> ByteBuffer;
	mint nBytes = ByteBuffer.f_GetLen();
~~~
	</td>
	<td>It does not mean that the variable is a number, it is only applicable if you can replace the n with "number of". </td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>p</td>
	<td>Pointer</td>
	<td>
~~~
	TCVector<uint8> ByteBuffer;
	uint8 const *pBytes = ByteBuffer.f_GetArray();
	TCUniquePointer<uint32> pInstance = fg_Construct();
~~~
	</td>
	<td>Both real pointers and smart-pointers are assumed to follow the pointer concept</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>f</td>
	<td>Function object</td>
	<td>
~~~
	typedef void (FTest)(int);
	class CTest
	{
		TCFunction<void ()> m_fTest;
		FTest *m_fTest2;
	};
 
	void fg_Test(CTest const &_Test)
	{
		auto fTest = []()
			{
				_Test.m_fTest();
				if (_Test.m_fTest2)
					_Test.m_fTest2();
			}
		;
		fTest();
	}
~~~
	</td>
	<td>The function object concept applies to both function pointers and functors.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>r</td>
	<td>Range</td>
	<td>
~~~
	TCVector<uint8> ByteBuffer;
	for (auto rByte = fg_Range(ByteBuffer); rByte; ++rByte)
		*rByte;
~~~
	</td>
	<td>When applied to a variable the r concept prefix means a iterator range, not to be confused with the r prefix for a function.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>r</td>
	<td>Recursive function</td>
	<td>
~~~
	void fg_rTest(uint32 _Value)
	{
		if (_Value > 0)
			fg_rTest(_Value - 1);
	}
~~~
	</td>
	<td>When applied to a function the r concept prefix means recursive, not to be confused with the r prefix for a variable.</td>
</tr>
</table>
 
 
*/

